      "text": "To solve for the number of possible values of \\( k \\) in the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\) given that both roots are prime numbers, we use the factored form of the quadratic equation. Let the roots be \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), where both \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are prime numbers. Then we can write the quadratic equation as:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - (p+q)x + pq = 0.\n\\]\n\nBy comparing this to the original equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\), we can identify the following relationships:\n\\[\np + q = 63\n\\]\n\\[\npq = k.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find all pairs of prime numbers \\( (p, q) \\) that satisfy \\( p + q = 63 \\). To do this, we consider the possible values for \\( p \\) and check which pairs are prime numbers and sum to 63.\n\nFirst, observe that the sum of two primes cannot be an even number larger than \\( 63 \\) because the sum of an even prime and an even number is still even. The only odd prime number less than 63 is 5, and the only possible even prime number is 2. But since \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are both prime and their sum is 63, one prime must be even (2) and the other must be 63 - 2 = 61. However, 61 is a prime number.\n\nThus, the only pair meeting the requirement is \\( (2, 61) \\). Now we calculate \\( k \\) using the product of these primes:\n\\[\nk = pq = 2 \\times 61 = 122.\n\\]\n\nSince there is only one pair of prime numbers that satisfy the given conditions, there is only one possible value for \\( k \\).\n\nThus, the number of possible values of \\( k \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
