      "text": "We are given the quadratic equation ( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 ) with prime roots. To find the number of possible values for ( k ):\n\n1. **Understand the Roots Relationship:**\n   If ( x_1 ) and ( x_2 ) are the roots of the quadratic, by Vieta's formulas:\n   [\n   x_1 + x_2 = 63\n   ]\n   [\n   x_1 cdot x_2 = k\n   ]\n\n2. **Check for Prime Roots:**\n   Iterate over all pairs of prime numbers that sum up to ( 63 ). For each pair ((p, q)) where (p + q = 63), compute ( p cdot q ).\n\n3. **Count Valid ( k ) Values:**\n   Count each unique value of ( k ) obtained from the products of prime pairs.\n\nPutting this into Chain-of-Thought rationale:\n\n- Iterate over all pairs of prime numbers ( p ) and ( q ) such that ( p + q = 63 ).\n- For each pair, compute the product ( p cdot q ).\n- Collect all unique products as they represent the number of possible values of ( k ).\n\nThe answer is the count of distinct ( k ) values obtained from the pairs of prime numbers summing up to ( 63 ).\n\n[ python\ndef count_possible_values_of_k(prime_sum):\n    possible_k = set()\n    prime_numbers = get_prime_numbers_up_to(prime_sum)\n    \n    for p in prime_numbers:\n        q = prime_sum - p\n        if q in prime_numbers:\n            possible_k.add(p * q)\n    \n    return len(possible_k)\n]\n\nThe provided Python program uses the concept of prime numbers and sum conditions to determine the number of possible values for ( k ). The algorithm relies on the mathematical properties of prime numbers and the relationship between the sum and product of their pairs. \n\nFor the function `get_prime_numbers_up_to` and `count_possible_values_of_k` to provide efficient and accurate results, the implementation necessitates an understanding of prime number generation and interested in prime pairs summing up to a specified integer. \n\nThe step-by-step rationale clearly outlines the process, dissection, and computation needed to determine the number of possible values for ( k ):\n```python\ndef count_possible_values_of_k(prime_sum):\n    possible_k = set()\n    prime_numbers = get_prime_numbers_up_to(prime_sum)\n    \n    for p in prime_numbers:\n        q = prime_sum - p\n        if q in prime_numbers:\n            possible_k.add(p * q)\n    \n    return len(possible_k)\n```",
